out.push(SAMPLE(['Mrs Oueidat said the one rule is that nothing in the page may instruct the person who opens it: no remember, no do not forget, no I hope you will have. Mr Shihabi asked why not, since he will be the one reading it, and Mrs Oueidat said that is exactly why: an instruction to yourself in five years is a resolution, and a resolution tells the person who opens it what the writer wanted, and they already know that because they are the writer, and what they do not know is what was true. Mrs Khoury said a page made of plans has the same fault in a politer form, because I intend to publish two papers is settled by whether you did, which is a fact about your diary. Dr Qabbani said so the page is predictions, and predictions about things you cannot settle today, and the test is one question: could I find out the answer this afternoon? Mrs Tarabishi said hers is about referees and she has no idea, and then it is the best kind, and the mechanism is what makes it interesting because a prediction with a reason attached fails informatively. Mr Tayara said a reason attached sounds like a hedge, and Mrs Khoury said it is the opposite: a hedge makes a prediction unfalsifiable and a mechanism makes it falsifiable twice. Mrs Ajouz said hers is about an office and not about her, and those are the ones that come out best. Mrs Rizk asked how many sentences, and three hundred words is the band and the shape is two or three claims, the mechanism for each, the state of things today, a date, and a keeper. Mr Zahreddine asked why not a friend, and because a friend will give it back early if you ask, and you will ask. And Mrs Oueidat said the thing to say at the end and not before: it is the only document in Book 2 with no reader but you, and the whole of the rest of the course has been about writing for somebody who can refuse you. Mrs Khoury said that is why it is harder and not easier, because a reader who cannot refuse you cannot correct you either, and every protection the course has built is a protection against a reader who will answer back. Mrs Tarabishi said the mechanism is the only protection left, because a claim with a mechanism can be wrong in two places and a claim without one cannot be wrong usefully at all. And Mrs Oueidat said the thing she will take away is the keeper, because the keeper is the only part of the arrangement that protects the date from the person who set it.']));
out.push(A('⚠ **The last line is the one to carry out of the cycle and it goes at the end of the lesson and not before: it is the only document in Book 2 with no reader but you.** **The whole of the rest of the course has been about writing for somebody who can refuse you** — **and Mrs Khoury\'s gloss is the part that makes it teachable: a reader who cannot refuse you cannot correct you either, so the mechanism is the only protection left.**'));
out.push(S('Board clinic – typical mistakes'));
out.push(A('**1** ⚠ _Remember that you were right about this._ → **an instruction; a resolution and not evidence.**   **2** _I intend to publish two papers._ → **a plan; a calendar with feelings in it.**   **3** ⚠ **A claim settleable this afternoon** → **half of all first claims, and every one a thing the writer had not asked for.**   **4** **No mechanism** → **when it fails it teaches nothing.**   **5** ⚠ **A softener** → **three a page on average, and back in the second draft in six of ten.**   **6** **A friend as keeper** → **the date has no protection and October is coming.**'));
out.push(S('Stage 5 · Read'));
out.push(A('**1** ⚠ **Four people in the department cite a register they cannot produce and none of them has written to the graduate school about it** — **and an office that is asked once by an outsider moves faster than one asked four times by its own.** **She records at 15.7\'s distance that the mechanism rests on one case in another department, which is a denominator of one.**'));
out.push(A('**2** ⚠ **Whether she will still be doing research.** **It is the question she most wants answered and it is a question about her own diary** — **and a page that asked it would be settled by what she did, which whoever opens it will already know.**'));
out.push(A('**3** ⚠ **_Probably_ from claim one, _I think_ from claim two, and _in all likelihood_ from the state of things.** **Each one made a sentence that could not be wrong** — **and she records them because they came back twice, which is the measure of how hard a writer finds it to stay falsifiable.**'));
