out.push(SAMPLE(['Mrs Khoury said Mr Deeb gets the letter and it is two lines, because he has already corrected the record himself, politely, and the only thing owed to him is that the correction be accepted in writing. Mr Attar said what must not be said is that it was still a kindness, and that is exactly it: it was not a kindness, it was his office\'s routine, and a reply that insists on the kindness has told a man that his account of his own work is wrong because the writer prefers a different version. Mrs Oueidat said the writer made it the centre of the note, and Mr Attar said the centre, and a whole paragraph about two hands and two dates, and the paragraph is still true and the reason he gave for it was wrong. Dr Qabbani said so the finding stands and the reason falls, and Mrs Khoury said that is 15.6 arriving one cycle early and from the opposite direction: a prediction with a mechanism fails twice over, and here a finding with a mechanism has had its mechanism fail and the finding has held. Mrs Tarabishi asked what goes in the two lines, and the correction accepted and the finding restated without the wrong reason. Mr Tayara said that is better for him than the first version, and it is better for him and also truer, because a routine that produces unforgeable evidence for everybody who uses the registry is a larger thing than one clerk being thoughtful. Mrs Ajouz asked about the fourth sentence, which nobody has mentioned, and that is the finding of the cycle and it took three accounts and a week to surface. Mr Shihabi asked whether it matters, since it did not need to be acted on, and it matters because it is the sentence the writer got right and he has spent a week on the three he got wrong. Mr Zahreddine asked whether anybody is at fault at all, and nobody, and this is the second time in Book 2. And Mr Attar said the thing he will take away is that Mr Deeb corrected him: a registry clerk read a note from a stranger, found one sentence that was wrong about his own office, and wrote two lines, and that is the behaviour this whole course has been describing, from somebody who has not had a day of it. Mrs Khoury said the room should sit with that rather than be charmed by it, because the moves in this course are not a course’s property and a registry has been making unforgeable records since before anybody here was born. And Mrs Oueidat said the thing she will take away is the fourth sentence, because it was correctly written, it lapsed as it was built to, and three people read the letter and none of them mentioned it.']));
